#
# The tiles on screen are painted first, and only they hold back a
# rendering update's composite.
#
# A rendering update's composite waited until every tile the update
# scheduled, on every layer, was painted, and the main thread's next update
# waited for that composite. Dirty tiles went to the four painting threads in
# hash order, so the visible ones queued behind tiles off screen. During a
# fast fling on OSNews (three page-sized layers) a batch of 13-36 tiles held
# the next update back up to 190 ms while the page coasted onto tiles nobody
# had created yet: the screen went white. Painting itself was not slow
# (median 0.4 ms a tile, the slowest 29 ms); the waiting was.
#
# Now:
# - a layer's dirty tiles are painted in order: those on screen first (the
#   visible rect, reaching 50 ms of travel ahead, as the main thread's
#   visible rect trails a fling), then ahead of the scroll, nearest first,
#   then the rest of the cover by distance;
# - only the tiles on screen count for the composite of their update
#   (CoordinatedSceneState's pending tiles); one painted off screen asks for
#   a TileDrain composite, and SkiaBackingStore applies a tile's update once
#   its buffer is painted, keeping a still-painting one (and those after it)
#   for a later composite rather than showing it half-painted. The tile's
#   list is taken before any update is applied: applied in place (the first
#   version), a composite read an entry with no buffer and the compositor
#   thread crashed on the first page; what changed the list under the loop
#   was not found.
#
# Flinging fast down OSNews (Jolla Phone, with 0035): frames while coasting
# with more than 5% of the screen unpainted 6-7% -> 1-2%, the worst 69-79% ->
# 15-57%; rendering updates 26-32 -> 41-43 a second; the web compositor's
# missed frames 3-4% -> 1%.
#
